What is an Automotive Steering Torque & Angle Sensor?
Automotive steering torque and angle sensors are dual‑purpose electromechanical devices that serve as the primary input for electronic power steering (EPS) and a wide array of ADAS functions, including lane‑keeping assist, adaptive cruise control, and autonomous parking. Typically mounted on the steering column, rack or base, the sensors capture two essential parameters: the driver‑applied torque (a measure of effort) and the rotational angle of the wheel. Data are converted into high‑speed digital signals that feed the vehicle’s electronic control unit (ECU), enabling real‑time motor‑assist calculations, stability control, and predictive steering algorithms. Since 2026, a growing proportion of models incorporate full redundancy and meet ISO 26262 ASIL D safety standards, positioning these sensors as critical safety nodes rather than ancillary components.
